4.19.m:
Fleeing in fear, he went to the Yamunā pool.
Because of Saubhari's curse, the lord of birds was unable to go to that [pool];
the serpent stayed there in fear, and his hosts went [there] afterwards. / 121

Frightened by the sage's gaze, the fish fell into the water.
And the lord of sages spoke to him [Garuḍa] who was preparing to take it again;
and the fish, out of terror of Garuḍa, remained near the sage. / 128

4.23.m:
I will bring you satisfaction upon returning from the moon's abode;
having adorned myself for the Moon's sake, today is the rendezvous for him, his passionate woman.
By the mere embrace of another, there will be a violation of duty;

and the life of those who do not protect duty is in vain.
Those women who do not know the embrace of the Moon are pronounced fools;
they remain as if in their mother's womb, undiscerning of masculine delights.

meditating on the lotus feet of Kṛṣṇa, the Paramātman.
The gaze of the two lovers did not fall upon the great sage standing nearby;
for those driven by lust have no awareness, their minds destroyed by passion. 128

Having spoken thus and wept, she took refuge with the sage.
To some on this earth, awareness comes only through calamity;
and seeing their distress, the sage was moved to compassion. 142
